1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,337
Concrete is not a single substance.

2
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:07,813
It is a mixture in which one component is
chemically active and the rest are held.

3
00:00:07,813 --> 00:00:13,133
The active part is Portland cement, a
powder made by burning limestone and clay

4
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:13,739
together.

5
00:00:13,739 --> 00:00:18,536
Add water and the powder reacts, growing a
gel that fills the spaces between

6
00:00:18,536 --> 00:00:21,819
particles of sand and stone and hardens
around them.

7
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:26,155
Ask what concrete is made of and the
honest answer has two halves.

8
00:00:26,155 --> 00:00:30,757
By volume it is mostly rock that was
already hard before anyone touched it.

9
00:00:30,757 --> 00:00:35,213
By behaviour it is the thin layer of
chemistry wrapped around that rock,

10
00:00:35,213 --> 00:00:39,235
because the rock is not what sets and the
rock is not what fails.

11
00:00:39,235 --> 00:00:40,957
What is concrete made of?

12
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:45,754
Portland cement starts as limestone and
clay, ground together and fed into a

13
00:00:45,754 --> 00:00:46,637
rotating kiln.

14
00:00:46,637 --> 00:00:50,572
The kiln heats the mixture to roughly
1,450 degrees Celsius.

15
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:55,425
At that temperature the limestone
decomposes, releasing carbon dioxide and

16
00:00:55,425 --> 00:01:00,605
leaving calcium oxide, which then reacts
with silica and alumina from the clay.

17
00:01:00,605 --> 00:01:04,312
The material partly melts and fuses into
nodules called clinker.

18
00:01:04,312 --> 00:01:08,888
The clinker is cooled, ground to a fine
powder, and mixed with a few percent of

19
00:01:08,888 --> 00:01:12,248
gypsum to control how fast it will later
react with water.

20
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:17,501
What comes out of that kiln is not a
mineral that exists in nature in this

21
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:22,122
It is a manufactured set of calcium
silicates held in an unstable state by

22
00:01:22,122 --> 00:01:26,243
rapid cooling, and its whole usefulness is
that it wants to react.

23
00:01:26,243 --> 00:01:27,971
The rapid cooling matters.

24
00:01:27,971 --> 00:01:33,354
If the clinker cooled slowly the calcium
silicates would relax toward more stable

25
00:01:33,354 --> 00:01:36,477
arrangements and lose much of their
reactivity.

26
00:01:36,477 --> 00:01:38,916
Quenching traps them in the reactive form.

27
00:01:38,916 --> 00:01:43,270
This is the same principle used in
hardening steel, applied to a completely

28
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:47,856
different chemistry: the useful state is
not the stable one, so the material is

29
00:01:47,856 --> 00:01:51,397
frozen on the way to stability rather than
allowed to arrive.

30
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:53,948
The gypsum added at grinding is a brake.

31
00:01:53,948 --> 00:01:59,241
Without it, one of the clinker compounds
reacts with water almost instantly and the

32
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:01,600
mix stiffens before it can be placed.

33
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:06,608
The most common misunderstanding about
concrete is that it dries. It does not.

34
00:02:06,608 --> 00:02:11,936
It hydrates. The water becomes part of new
solid compounds rather than leaving.

35
00:02:11,936 --> 00:02:17,303
The main reaction is between water and the
calcium silicates, and it produces two

36
00:02:17,303 --> 00:02:22,338
things: calcium silicate hydrate, a gel
with no fixed crystal structure, and

37
00:02:22,338 --> 00:02:25,253
calcium hydroxide, which does form
crystals.

38
00:02:25,253 --> 00:02:26,681
The gel is the binder.

39
00:02:26,681 --> 00:02:31,873
It grows outward from each cement grain,
meets the gel growing from neighbouring

40
00:02:31,873 --> 00:02:33,365
grains, and interlocks.

41
00:02:33,365 --> 00:02:38,097
Because this is a reaction and not an
evaporation, concrete sets underwater, and

42
00:02:38,097 --> 00:02:43,005
concrete left to dry too fast in the sun
is weaker than concrete kept wet, which is

43
00:02:43,005 --> 00:02:46,021
the opposite of what the word drying would
predict.

44
00:02:46,021 --> 00:02:50,111
Hydration is the chemical incorporation of
water into a compound.

45
00:02:50,111 --> 00:02:53,824
In cement chemistry the water is not a
solvent that leaves.

46
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:55,528
It is a reagent that stays.

47
00:02:55,528 --> 00:03:00,767
This is why curing means keeping concrete
moist rather than keeping it dry, and why

48
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:05,753
a slab covered and kept damp for a week is
stronger than an identical slab left

49
00:03:05,753 --> 00:03:06,384
uncovered.

50
00:03:06,384 --> 00:03:08,792
The reaction does not finish quickly.

51
00:03:08,792 --> 00:03:14,258
Most strength arrives in the first month,
but the interior of a large pour continues

52
00:03:14,258 --> 00:03:19,139
to hydrate for years wherever unreacted
cement and water remain in contact.

53
00:03:19,139 --> 00:03:24,728
The single strongest predictor of concrete
strength is the ratio of water to cement

54
00:03:24,728 --> 00:03:25,267
by mass.

55
00:03:25,267 --> 00:03:28,845
More water makes the mix easier to place
and weaker once set.

56
00:03:28,845 --> 00:03:34,942
Lower ratios, near 0.35, give higher
strength; a workable structural mix

57
00:03:34,942 --> 00:03:41,038
commonly sits near 0.45; and ratios
approaching 0.70 give markedly lower

58
00:03:41,038 --> 00:03:41,800
strength.

59
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,103
The reason is space.

60
00:03:43,103 --> 00:03:48,447
Only part of the mix water is consumed by
hydration; the rest occupies volume, and

61
00:03:48,447 --> 00:03:51,640
when it eventually leaves it leaves behind
pores.

62
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,170
Every pore is a place where the gel is
not.

63
00:03:54,170 --> 00:03:58,876
Strength falls as porosity rises, so the
water that made the mix easy to pour is

64
00:03:58,876 --> 00:04:02,995
the same water that leaves the holes the
concrete later fails through.

65
00:04:02,995 --> 00:04:07,733
Full hydration of the cement requires
roughly 0.42 units of water per unit of

66
00:04:07,733 --> 00:04:12,778
cement by mass, counting both the water
chemically bound and the water held in the

67
00:04:12,778 --> 00:04:13,763
gel's own pores.

68
00:04:13,763 --> 00:04:18,716
Mixes are often batched wetter than that
for workability, and the surplus is what

69
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:20,061
forms capillary pores.

70
00:04:20,061 --> 00:04:25,335
Mixes batched drier than that cannot fully
hydrate on their own, which is why very

71
00:04:25,335 --> 00:04:30,287
low-ratio concretes depend on careful
curing and on admixtures that keep them

72
00:04:30,287 --> 00:04:32,216
placeable without extra water.

73
00:04:32,216 --> 00:04:34,730
The number is not a target to hit exactly.

74
00:04:34,730 --> 00:04:39,638
It is the line that explains why adding
water on site, to make a stiff load easier

75
00:04:39,638 --> 00:04:43,528
to handle, quietly lowers the strength of
the finished structure.

76
00:04:43,528 --> 00:04:47,518
The questions worth chasing are the ones
where the answer changes how you

77
00:04:47,518 --> 00:04:48,611
understand a system.

78
00:04:48,611 --> 00:04:53,890
By volume, aggregate is the majority of
ordinary concrete: coarse stone and sand

79
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:58,707
together typically occupy far more of the
mix than the cement paste does.

80
00:04:58,707 --> 00:05:03,204
It is tempting to call aggregate filler,
as though it were there to make the cement

81
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:03,800
go further.

82
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:04,913
That is backwards.

83
00:05:04,913 --> 00:05:09,921
Aggregate is usually stronger and stiffer
than the paste around it, and it is far

84
00:05:09,921 --> 00:05:11,528
more dimensionally stable.

85
00:05:11,528 --> 00:05:15,862
Cement paste shrinks as it hydrates and
dries; aggregate does not.

86
00:05:15,862 --> 00:05:20,131
A concrete that was all paste would crack
from its own shrinkage.

87
00:05:20,131 --> 00:05:22,302
The stone restrains that movement.

88
00:05:22,302 --> 00:05:27,155
Cost is a real reason to use aggregate,
but it is not the structural reason.

89
00:05:27,155 --> 00:05:31,970
Where the paste meets each stone there is
a thin region with a different structure

90
00:05:31,970 --> 00:05:36,315
from the bulk paste, more porous and
richer in calcium hydroxide crystals.

91
00:05:36,315 --> 00:05:40,466
This interfacial zone is generally the
weakest part of ordinary concrete, and

92
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:43,701
cracks tend to run through it rather than
through the stone.

93
00:05:43,701 --> 00:05:48,783
So the strength of concrete is often
limited not by the stone and not by the

94
00:05:48,783 --> 00:05:51,592
bulk gel but by the boundary between them.

95
00:05:51,592 --> 00:05:56,187
Improving that boundary, rather than
adding more cement, is where a great deal

96
00:05:56,187 --> 00:05:58,072
of concrete technology has gone.

97
00:05:58,072 --> 00:06:03,550
Ordinary structural concrete carries
compression well and tension badly.

98
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:09,865
A typical structural mix reaches about 30
megapascals in compression and about 3 in

99
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:16,104
tension, roughly a tenth as much, while
the Grade 60 reinforcing bar placed inside

100
00:06:16,104 --> 00:06:19,528
it yields at 420 megapascals under ASTM
A615.

101
00:06:19,528 --> 00:06:23,847
Under compression, cracks are pressed
closed and load transfers through the

102
00:06:23,847 --> 00:06:24,941
aggregate skeleton.

103
00:06:24,941 --> 00:06:30,475
Under tension, cracks open, and a material
full of pores and interfacial weaknesses

104
00:06:30,475 --> 00:06:33,608
has no shortage of places for a crack to
start.

105
00:06:33,608 --> 00:06:37,716
This asymmetry is why concrete is almost
always reinforced.

106
00:06:37,716 --> 00:06:43,147
Steel bars are placed where the engineer
expects tension, and the concrete and

107
00:06:43,147 --> 00:06:48,856
steel work as one member: concrete takes
the compression, steel takes the tension.

108
00:06:48,856 --> 00:06:53,635
The bond between them depends on the steel
staying passivated by the highly alkaline

109
00:06:53,635 --> 00:06:58,301
pore water, which is why cracks that let
in chloride or carbon dioxide are treated

110
00:06:58,301 --> 00:07:00,861
as structural problems and not cosmetic
ones.

111
00:07:00,861 --> 00:07:06,403
The alkalinity is a consequence of the
calcium hydroxide produced during

112
00:07:06,403 --> 00:07:09,340
It keeps a passive oxide film on the
steel.

113
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:15,077
Carbon dioxide from the air slowly reacts
with that calcium hydroxide and lowers the

114
00:07:15,077 --> 00:07:20,336
pH, a process called carbonation, which
advances inward from the surface over

115
00:07:20,336 --> 00:07:20,883
decades.

116
00:07:20,883 --> 00:07:26,147
When it reaches the steel, the passive
film is no longer maintained and corrosion

117
00:07:26,147 --> 00:07:26,797
can begin.

118
00:07:26,797 --> 00:07:32,386
Rust occupies more volume than the steel
it replaced, so it splits the concrete

119
00:07:32,386 --> 00:07:33,235
from inside.

120
00:07:33,235 --> 00:07:38,179
Most reinforced concrete that fails with
age fails this way, or through chlorides

121
00:07:38,179 --> 00:07:42,147
reaching the steel, rather than by the
concrete being overloaded.

122
00:07:42,147 --> 00:07:47,013
Roman concrete used lime and volcanic ash
rather than Portland cement, and some

123
00:07:47,013 --> 00:07:51,325
Roman marine structures have stood in
seawater for two thousand years.

124
00:07:51,325 --> 00:07:56,297
Published analyses of samples from Roman
harbour concrete have reported the growth

125
00:07:56,297 --> 00:08:01,026
of aluminous tobermorite and a related
mineral within the material, associated

126
00:08:01,026 --> 00:08:04,845
with seawater reacting with the volcanic
ash over long periods.

127
00:08:04,845 --> 00:08:10,092
That is close to the opposite of modern
behaviour, where seawater is a durability

128
00:08:10,092 --> 00:08:10,611
problem.

129
00:08:10,611 --> 00:08:14,381
It is worth being careful about what this
does and does not show.

130
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:19,307
Roman marine concrete is not stronger than
modern structural concrete, and it was not

131
00:08:19,307 --> 00:08:23,885
reinforced with steel, so it never faced
the failure mode that ends most modern

132
00:08:23,885 --> 00:08:25,160
reinforced structures.

133
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:29,921
What it demonstrates is that a slower
chemistry, given centuries, can keep

134
00:08:29,921 --> 00:08:32,173
changing in a direction that helps.

135
00:08:32,173 --> 00:08:36,336
The through-line is that concrete's
properties are set by the paste and

136
00:08:36,336 --> 00:08:39,795
limited by its pores, while its volume is
mostly aggregate.

137
00:08:39,795 --> 00:08:45,104
Water is the variable that connects them:
enough to complete hydration, and every

138
00:08:45,104 --> 00:08:46,808
extra unit becomes a pore.

139
00:08:46,808 --> 00:08:50,899
Setting is a reaction, not a drying, which
is why curing is wet.

140
00:08:50,899 --> 00:08:55,248
Strength in compression comes from an
aggregate skeleton bound by gel; weakness

141
00:08:55,248 --> 00:08:59,543
in tension comes from the same pores and
interfacial zones that the water left

142
00:08:59,543 --> 00:08:59,928
behind.

143
00:08:59,928 --> 00:09:04,077
Steel covers the tension, and the
alkalinity that protects the steel is

144
00:09:04,077 --> 00:09:08,634
itself a by-product of the hydration
reaction, which is why the chemistry that

145
00:09:08,634 --> 00:09:13,075
makes concrete work is also the chemistry
that determines how long it lasts.

146
00:09:13,075 --> 00:09:16,176
The numbers here are ranges, not
constants.

147
00:09:16,176 --> 00:09:21,730
Compressive strength depends on mix
design, aggregate, admixtures, curing and

148
00:09:21,730 --> 00:09:27,067
age, and structural concretes are
specified across a wide band rather than

149
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:28,365
at a single value.

150
00:09:28,365 --> 00:09:33,180
The water-cement figure for full hydration
is a calculated quantity that varies with

151
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:37,880
how the gel's own pore water is counted,
and different textbooks state it slightly

152
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:38,568
differently.

153
00:09:38,568 --> 00:09:43,633
Carbonation rates depend on exposure,
cover depth and concrete quality and are

154
00:09:43,633 --> 00:09:47,789
estimated statistically rather than
measured on every structure.

155
00:09:47,789 --> 00:09:52,381
The Roman marine concrete work is based on
a limited number of sampled sites.

156
00:09:52,381 --> 00:09:57,936
What is not in doubt is the identity of
the reaction, the role of the water-cement

157
00:09:57,936 --> 00:10:00,984
ratio, and the tension-compression
asymmetry.

158
00:10:00,984 --> 00:10:06,404
Concrete is mostly stone that was already
strong, held together by a gel that grows

159
00:10:06,404 --> 00:10:09,409
in water and leaves holes where the water
was.

160
00:10:09,409 --> 00:10:14,045
Almost everything about how it performs
follows from that one sentence.
